  "prompt_text": "The figure is a world map summarizing environmental impacts associated with global trade and consumption. It links different regions with arrows and icons to show where products are consumed, where waste ends up, and where pollution or resource use occurs. Across the top, long arrows labeled logistics CO₂ indicate that transporting goods by air and sea adds carbon emissions over long distances.\n\nThe map suggests a strong flow from Asia, especially East and Southeast Asia, toward Europe, North America, and Australia. Near Asia, warning symbols and labels highlight CO₂, water use, and chemical or agricultural pollution, implying that production burdens are concentrated there. Blue arrows connect this region to distant consumer markets, emphasizing that water use and wastewater are tied to manufacturing for export.\n\nGreen bag icons mark consumption in several wealthy regions, while trash-can icons indicate waste generation or disposal in North America, Europe, Africa, and Australia. A purple arrow toward Africa appears to represent imported waste, and an upward green arrow in South America indicates an increase. Overall, the image conveys that global supply chains shift pollution, water demand, and waste across continents rather than keeping impacts where goods are ultimately used.",
